  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nThe Erd\u0151s discrepancy problem requires minimizing the maximum overlap of a step function \\(h(x)\\) with its shifted complement \\(1 - h(x+k)\\) at all possible shifts \\(k\\). The core idea is to express the optimization problem in terms of discrete calculus and use sparse convex combinations to find binary solutions that minimize this maximum overlap. Unlike gradient-based methods, this approach leverages combinatorial structure to identify promising seed points and systematically refines them, avoiding common pitfalls like slow convergence in flat regions and local optima trapping. The rational penalty formulation allows selecting high-frequency components that drive improvement, effectively abandoning greedy frequency selection for dynamically adaptive optimization.\n\nMathematical Specification\nWith \\(x_i \\in [0, 2]\\) equally spaced, \\(h \\in \\mathbb{R}^n\\) subject to \\(\\sum_{i=1}^n h_i \\Delta x = 1\\) (where \\(\\Delta x = 2/n\\)), and \\(0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1\\) for all \\(i\\). The objective is to minimize:\n\\[\nC_5 = \\max_k \\left( \\frac{1}{\\Delta x} \\cdot \\text{overlap}(h, h(\\cdot - k))\n\\]\nThe reformulation uses discrete calculus: define \\(Dh_i = h_{i-1} - h_i\\) to identify abrupt transitions. Domain attention focuses on \\(l = 1\\) to \\(n-1\\), where overlaps occur meaningfully. The optimization uses the calculus-based coordinate descent.\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. **Initial Construction**: Start with `initial_h_values` provided or build one using combinatorial selection with projection.\n2. **Sparse Optimization**:\n   - Use a sparse subset \\(S \\subseteq \\{0, 1, \\ldots, n-1\\}\\) with \\(|S| = O(\\sqrt{n})\\) and derive \\(h_i = a_i\\) where \\(a_i = 0\\) or \\(1\\) unless near \\(0.5\\).\n   - Formulate optimization as a SPORADIC minimization with standard constraints.\n3. **Penalty Handling**:\n   - Define rational penalty: \\(\\rho(h) = \\|L_2\\text{ residuals}\\|_2^2\\)\n   - Adjust penalty adaptively based on convergence rate\n4. **Binary Enforcement**:\n   - Use rounded solution \\(b_i = \\text{round}(h_i)\\) with integral adjustment\n5. **Localized Optimization**:\n   - Update estimates using sparse differences: \\(\\Delta h_i = -\\text{sign}(Dh_i) \\cdot \\tau \\cdot \\gamma\\)\n   - Apply iterative reweighting with penalty coefficients\n6. **Fallback Mechanism**:\n   - If divergence, revert to previous best candidate; otherwise report solution\n\nSearch Choices and Variations\n- **Intial Construction Methods**: \n  - Combinatorial: use selection with integral projection if available \n  - Trivial fallback: take fixed binary pattern with `n_init = 600`\n- **Penalty Coefficient Strategy**:\n  - Start small (\u223c0.1) and increase adaptively based on local improvement rate\n  - Key parameters: \\(\\alpha = 0.5, \\beta = 0.9\\) for penalty growth\n- **Diversity Management**:\n  - Random perturbations with combinatorial termination criteria if progress stalls\n  - Dynamic restarts for dimensional refinements every 150 seconds\n\nCompute Budget and Robustness\n- With \\(n = 600\\), the algorithm demandsresolution-sensitive scaling with expected 12 CPU-hours total computation\n- Eary termination checks after 5 hours (\\(\u22482.4e6\\) function evaluations) \n- Integral projection ensures feasibility via \\(\\sum h_i = n/2\\)\n\nValidation and Return Contract\n1. **Feasibility**: \\(0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1\\) exactly and \\(\\text{compute\\_corr}\\) must stabilize\n2. **Objective**: C\u2085 computed as \\(\\max_k \\text{overlap}\\) times \\(\\Delta x\\)\n3. **Output Format**: `(numpy.array, c5_bound, n_points)`",
